\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A fixed charge map on two real products}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a finite circle choose its axis as the preserved axis,
with a single complex spin line of direction #{+\bbI}.
The inversion of \ref{fdip-013T} fixes the sites and is the
identity on spin. Use trivial links and use coefficient
conjugation on the base field. Let #{E=\mathbb C^2} have the
standard orthonormal basis and fix both channel labels.
Take #{Y_1=\operatorname{diag}(1,\sqrt{2})} and #{Y_2=\bbI_E}.
Their products #{A=\operatorname{diag}(1,2)} and #{B=\bbI_E}
are real, so coefficient conjugation on #{E} preserves both.}

\p{Choose instead #{j_E(z_1,z_2)=(\bar z_2,\bar z_1)}.
This antiunitary also squares to #{\bbI_E}. Its conjugated
first product is #{\operatorname{diag}(2,1)}. Thus all word
traces are real, and a multiplicity charge satisfying the fixed
pair exists, while this particular choice in \ref{fdip-0196}
carries the first product to a different destination.}
